196	The update on the topic of HTML and the recent video update discusses the content of the freeze. We'll start by talking about HTML. I previously mentioned some important hints, and I need to add content in a drawable file. The description must remain in the format of an "On map one," and I will be using the term HTML as an abbreviation, which represents Hypertext Markup Language.\n\nWhen we talk about any language, we mean that there is a specific meaning associated with it. For example, the word "Hello" means "welcome," and so on. Each language comprises a set of defined meanings, and each expression within that language has a specific meaning. In this language, you will find a collection of keywords, and each keyword has a particular function, form, or meaning.\n\nIn HTML, the meaning of markup is simple. Imagine you have a book and I ask you to underline and highlight the important points that you need to remember—like how you might highlight the main title or underline a term you find interesting. Thus, marking or creating markup means adding specific attributes to certain content. For example, you could set some content to be hidden (as a title for a webpage), bold other content, italicize another, and emphasize more in various ways.\n\nThe term "Hypertext" refers to the ability to navigate between different pieces of text, video, or audio data without needing to consume everything sequentially. If you had a book and I asked you to read it straight through, you would need to start from the first page to the last. However, if we introduce "Hyper," as seen in platforms like Wikipedia, specific highlighted words link to different pages, allowing you to navigate freely without reading the entire article.\n\nEssentially, HTML will enable you to create "hyperlinks"—places you can click to jump to different pages or sections. A markup language highlights specific elements, and in HTML, you create a document that contains both text and navigation abilities.\n\nNow, if I want to write in HTML, I first need an editor. The simplest one is Notepad, available by default on your operating system. There are many other editors like Sublime Text, Notepad++, and PHPStorm, each with different features, some of which are free.\n\nWhen you write code, it will be stored in a file that must have an appropriate extension. For example, an audio file typically has a .mp3 extension, while a video file may use .mp4. The HTML extension is either .html or .htm, and you'll be working within these file formats.\n\nLet's discuss setting up HTML using the PHPStorm editor. Suppose you navigate to a designated folder, and by right-clicking, you create a new file, selecting the HTML File type, and naming it "index." We choose this name because when you upload a website to a web server, it's set to look for a file named "index" by default.\n\nYou might wonder if it's acceptable to name files differently or if there are naming conventions. It’s generally recommended that the filenames are clear and descriptive, using lowercase letters and either underscores or hyphens for separation, and avoiding spaces for best practices.\n\nOnce you've opened the file, you will encounter lines of code that include tags such as <html>, <head>, and <body>—which indicate how the content is structured. The errors and structures here form what is known as elements in HTML.\n\nAn element contains a tag, content, and attributes. Tags are keywords enclosed in angle brackets. For instance, the opening tag and closing tag indicate the start and end of an element, with the content lying between them. Attributes provide additional features or information to those elements and are written alongside the opening tag.\n\nThere are two classifications of elements: those with both opening and closing tags (open-close elements), and self-closing tags, which do not require an end tag. Each element features tags as well as content and attributes.\n\nHTML is largely hierarchical or tree-like, which means you might have elements containing other elements, leading to nested structures, termed the DOM (Document Object Model).\n\nUnderstanding the basic setup of an HTML document requires recognizing that the opening line specifies the HTML version being used (like HTML5), confirming to the browser that the document is in fact HTML5. It’s important to contain everything within <html> tags and to have <head> and <body> sections—where the head holds metadata and the body contains the visible content for users.\n\nWith the <head> section, you'll set various pieces of information for the browser, include external libraries, and define the title of the page, while the <body> will eventually contain all the text and elements visible to the user.\n\nOne common element is the <meta> tag, which provides character set information like UTF-8, ensuring that the browser can correctly process text in various languages. You can also define language dictionaries for different characters based on the content you wish to present.\n\nTo summarize, while working with HTML, it's essential to understand the foundational elements related to tags, content, and attributes, including the difference between block-level and inline elements. These will establish the structure and behavior of your webpages as you begin to write code in HTML.
