2425	4c209abd-5ac2-4e56-a0f7-6ccc01c55709	other	Good evening, and welcome to a Briton Brief Exclusive. Tonight... a developing crisis deep within Whitehall that threatens to blow a multi-billion-pound hole in the Government's economic strategy.\n\nRachel Reeves’ highly controversial, and deeply unpopular, plan to slap a 'pay-per-mile' tax on British motorists is on the brink of a spectacular collapse. Rather than filling the nation's empty coffers, Number 10 has been formally warned that this punitive levy could end up costing the Treasury a staggering four point eight billion pounds.\n\nIn a blistering letter delivered directly to the Exchequer, a formidable coalition of trade bodies—representing drivers, infrastructure firms, and renewable energy businesses—have branded the Chancellor’s policy a catastrophic, quote, "fiscal own goal."\n\nThe scheme, brutally outlined for the November twenty-twenty-five Budget and set to launch in April twenty-twenty-eight, will force electric vehicle owners to hand over three pence for every single mile they drive. Plug-in hybrids will be hit with a one-point-five pence charge.\n\nBut the evidence from abroad is utterly damning. The trade groups warned Exchequer Secretary Dan Tomlinson that similar stealth taxes deployed in Iceland and New Zealand triggered an immediate, catastrophic slump in sales—plummeting by up to seventy-five per cent.\n\nIndustry experts are sounding the alarm. If British buyers refuse to pay this new driving tax, the Treasury will face a massive, self-inflicted black hole in VAT receipts. Motorists will simply delay upgrading their vehicles, paralysing the market and inflicting a fatal blow to the UK economy.\n\nWhile the recent, tragic escalation of the Iran war has temporarily forced drivers towards EVs to escape roller-coaster petrol prices, industry leaders warn this tax raid will kill any long-term transition. As the Treasury stubbornly defends its policy, claiming it is investing in support for drivers, the working public is left asking one simple question: is this about a green transition, or is it just another desperate scheme to empty our pockets?\n\nThe war on the motorist, it seems, is entering its most expensive phase yet. Stay subscribed to The Briton Brief for the unvarnished truth.	fable		fable	1	mp3	bd4eae7f-8334-4a50-85d6-2ab9d2d5eaa8	COMPLETED	/app/media/audio_files/26344687-23cb-40fe-b877-f0c805c4d90f_4c209abd-5ac2-4e56-a0f7-6ccc01c55709.mp3		1903	2026-05-05 18:29:00.543869+00	2026-05-05 18:29:29.12493+00	26344687-23cb-40fe-b877-f0c805c4d90f
