3014	01f70d93-198d-4809-8afe-c53c125a45f1	other	Good evening, and welcome to a Briton Brief Exclusive. Tonight... sheer desperation inside Number 10.\n\nIn a move that has left political commentators and the public utterly stunned, Prime Minister Keir Starmer has just appointed Gordon Brown as his new Special Envoy on Global Finance. Yes, you heard that correctly.\n\nFollowing a catastrophic local election disaster where the Labour Party haemorrhaged over one thousand four hundred council seats, a panicked Prime Minister has desperately turned to the ghosts of New Labour to save his crumbling administration.\n\nBut let us take a short walk down memory lane and remember exactly who Gordon Brown is, and what his so-called financial "expertise" cost the British taxpayer.\n\nThis is the very same Chancellor of the Exchequer who committed one of the most unforgivable economic blunders in modern British history. Between nineteen-ninety-nine and two-thousand-and-two, Brown inexplicably decided to sell off more than half of Britain’s entire gold reserves—over three hundred and ninety-five tonnes of it.\n\nAnd the price he accepted? A rock-bottom two hundred and seventy-five dollars per ounce. It was a catastrophic fire sale that cost the Treasury billions of pounds in lost value, as global gold prices subsequently skyrocketed.\n\nReform UK leader Nigel Farage has already mercilessly mocked the appointment, stating that an unpopular Prime Minister now sees a man who famously lost a general election as his saviour, adding simply: "Labour are doomed".\n\nYet, this is the very man Starmer now trusts to establish multilateral finance mechanisms and boost our country's economic security.\n\nIs this a masterstroke of political experience, or the final, frantic thrashing of a government completely out of its depth?\n\nStay subscribed to The Briton Brief for the unvarnished truth.	fable		fable	1	mp3	fd7bc43c-6ba1-403d-ac3e-8b5898577c2a	COMPLETED	/app/media/audio_files/26344687-23cb-